   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the family court act and the domestic relations law, in\nrelation to establishment and modification of child support orders; and to\namend the social services law, in relation to an increase in the annual\nservice fee for child support services",
    "basePrintNo": "S6560",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6560-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§413 & 451, Fam Ct Act; amd §§240 & 236, Dom Rel L; amd §111-g, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Family Court Act",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6560\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the family court act and the domestic\nrelations law, in relation to establishment and modification of child\nsupport orders; and to amend the social services law, in relation to an\nincrease in the annual service fee for child support services\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the Domestic Relations Law (DRL) and the Family\nCourt Act (FCA) to comply with the provisions of the federal 2016 Flexi-\nbility, Efficiency and Modernization in Child Support Enforcement\nPrograms regarding the establishment or modification of child support\norders for incarcerated noncustodial parents. It would clarify that the\nincarceration of the child support obligor is not to be treated as\nvoluntary unemployment in either the establishment or modification of a\nsupport order unless such incarceration is the result of non-payment of\na child support order, or an offense against the custodial parent or\nchild who is the subject of the order or judgment. The bill provides\nthat incarceration shall not be a bar to establish a substantial change\nin circumstances, barring these exceptions.\n\nThis bill would also amend the Social Services Law (SSL) to comply with\nthe mandatory provision of the federal Bipartisan Budget Act of 2018 to\nincrease from $25 to $35 the annual service fee for providing child\nsupport services in the case of an individual who has never received\nTitle IV-A assistance. In addition, the bill would amend the SSL to\ncomply with the federal requirement to increase from $500 to $550 the\namount of support that the State must collect and disburse to the family\nprior to imposing the fee for each federal fiscal year.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend FCA § 413 to provide that incarcera-\ntion shall not be considered voluntary unemployment in establishing\nchild support obligations, except in certain situations.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend DRL § 240 to provide that incarcera-\ntion shall not be considered voluntary unemployment in establishing\nchild support obligations, except in certain situations.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would amend FCA § 451 to provide that incarcera-\ntion shall not be considered voluntary unemployment in modifying child\nsupport obligations and shall not be a bar to establish a substantial\nchange in circumstances, except in certain situations.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would amend Part B of DRL § 236 to provide that\nincarceration shall not be considered voluntary unemployment in modify-\ning child support obligations and shall not be a bar to establish a\nsubstantial change in circumstances, except in certain situations.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would amend SSL § 111-g to increase the annual\nservice fee imposed on persons who receive child support service s who\nhave never received assistance pursuant to Title IV-A of the federal\nSocial Security Act (SSA), from $25 to $35, and to increase the minimum\ncollection requirement from $500 to $550 per federal fiscal year.\n\n\nSection 6 of the bill would provide that the bill would take effect\nimmediately and would apply to any pending action or proceeding.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis would implement conforming changes to bring New York State law into\ncompliance with the federal regulatory amendments published in the Flex-\nibility, Efficiency and Modernization in Child Support Enforcement\nPrograms (\"Final Rule\"). The Final Rule provides that states may not\ntreat incarceration as voluntary unemployment in proceedings to estab-\nlish or modify a child support order. The bill implements this federal\nrequirement and provides that incarceration shall not be a bar to estab-\nlish a substantial change in circumstance s, unless such incarceration\nis the result of non-payment of a child support order, or an offense\nagainst the custodial parent or child who is the subject of the order or\njudgment. Because incarceration cannot be treated as voluntary unemploy-\nment (except in the enumerated circumstances), support awards for incar-\ncerated individuals will not be imputed from pre-incarceration earnings.\nInstead, modification would be based on the incarcerated individual's\ncurrent financial circumstances. If there is a basis for a modification\n(see FCA 451 \n3), the child support guidelines would be applied to the\nincarcerated individual's current income and assets to determine his or\nher new support obligation. While incarceration itself would not be\ngrounds for a modification, any significant reduction in the noncusto-\ndial parent's ability to pay support could be the basis of a downward\nmodification (except in the enumerated circumstances).\n\nIncarcerated parents usually have limited earning ability while in pris-\non. Addressing these circumstances at the establishment stage, or\nthrough modification of the child support order early in the incarcera-\ntion, is important considering that the accrual of significant child\nsupport debt during that period can interfere with employment success\nand lead to engagement with the underground economy or nonpayment of\nsupport. These parents currently leave incarceration with an average of\n$20,000 or more in unpaid child support* and annual earnings reduced by\n40 percent** This bill also seeks to ensure the right of all parents to\nseek a review of their child support order when circumstances change.\nRemoving the barriers to obtaining a modification during the period of\nincarceration would help ensure that orders for incarcerated parents are\nset based upon the obliger's ability to pay while that person is in\njail.\n\nThis bill would also amend SSL § 111-g which provides for child support\nservices to individuals not otherwise eligible by virtue of receipt of\npublic benefits. SSL § 111-g currently mandates the $25 annual service\nfee imposed on persons who receive child support services pursuant to\nthis section of law, who have never received assistance pursuant to\nTitle IV-A of the federal SSA, and to whom at least $500 of child\nsupport has been disbursed during the federal fiscal year. Section five\nof the bill amends SSL . 111-g to comply with a federal mandate requir-\ning the increase in the annual fee from $25 to $35 and the increase in\nthe minimum collection requirement for assessment of the fee from $500\nto $550 per federal fiscal year. The imposition of the annual service\nfee is a condition of continued eligibility for funding of the Title\nIV-D Child Support Program and the Temporary Assistance for Needy Fami-\nlies (TANF) block grant.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill. Fiscal implications:  Failure to comply with the\nTitle IV-D State Plan requirement to increase the annual support\ncollection fee by January 1, 2020 could subject the State to loss of 66\npercent federal financial participation for administrative expenditures\nby the Child Support Program and a penalty against the TANF block grant.\n\nThis federally-mandated change to the service fees is expected to\nincrease the annual amount of program fee revenue from custodial parents\nby $1.5 million, from $3.8 million to $5.3 million. The social services\ndistricts would retain 34 percent, or $1.8 million, of this revenue for\nadministrative relief, an increase of approximately $500,000 over\ncurrent levels. The remaining 66 percent of the fee revenue would be\nretained by the Federal Government.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe bill provides for an immediate effective date and would apply to any\npending action or proceeding.\n* National Conference of State Legislatures, Child Support and Incarcer-\nation, 2016 available at httpliwww.ncsi.orgiresearch/human-\nservicesichild-support-and- incarceration.aspx.\n** U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of Child Support\nEnforcement, Division of Policy and Training, \"Jobs not Jail Infograph-\nic\", published October 2015 on the OCSE website at\nhttps://www.acf.hhs.govisitesidefault/ffies/Programsicssijobs not rail\nfinal 10 02.pdf and Collateral Costs: Incarceration's Effect on Economic\nMobility. The Pew Charitable Trusts. (2010, September), available at:\nhttp://www.pewtrusts.oreimedia/legacv/uploadedfiles/pcs\nassets/2010/collateralcosts1pdf.pdf.\n",
